[18:12:17] [backend] ## ANSWER
[18:12:17] [backend] In my 2+ years at Morgan Stanley building Angular and React applications, I start wireframing by mapping the user journey and the top actions each screen must support. Then I create low-fidelity layouts for content hierarchy, validation states, and edge cases so product, QA, and backend teams align early. In enterprise modules, that approach helped me translate wireframes into reusable UI components and shared state logic, which reduced redundant code and improved consistency. I also map each section to Spring Boot API contracts upfront so frontend and backend can develop in parallel with fewer defects.
[18:12:17] [backend] 
[18:12:17] [backend] ## KEY POINTS
[18:12:17] [backend] - Start with user flow and key actions, not visuals first.
[18:12:17] [backend] - Mention 2+ years of enterprise Angular/React delivery and reuse-driven component design.
[18:12:17] [backend] - Tie wireframing to BeaconFire needs: maintainable code, faster collaboration, and lower defect risk.
[18:12:17] [backend] 
[18:12:17] [backend] ## CAUTION
[18:12:17] [backend] Don’t describe wireframing as just “drawing screens”; emphasize alignment, validation states, and engineering handoff.
