[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "behavioral",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Career progression at Morgan Stanley vendor teams plus current graduate study and continuous learning focus.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "My earlier titles were tied to project scope in a vendor setup, and now I am making a long-term growth move in the US market while finishing my MS. I am comfortable starting at entry level because I want to align with your engineering standards, stack, and delivery process from day one. My growth path is to earn larger ownership through consistent delivery, strong code quality, and reliable collaboration across sprints."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "Explain the four OOP principles in Java and give a real example of how you applied them in production code.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Spring Boot service development, reusable component design, and enterprise feature implementation.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "I use encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism to keep Java code modular and maintainable. In Spring Boot projects, I encapsulated business logic in service classes and used clear interfaces so controllers stayed focused on request handling. I used polymorphic interface-based components so implementations could change during API and security updates without breaking callers."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "When would you choose List vs Set vs Map in Java, and what tradeoffs matter for performance and correctness?",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Java backend development and performance-aware code quality work.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "I choose a List when order matters and duplicates are valid, a Set when uniqueness is required, and a Map when I need key-based lookup. For correctness, Set prevents accidental duplicates and Map makes relationships explicit. For performance, I usually start with ArrayList and HashMap, then adjust based on access pattern and profiling results."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "How do equals() and hashCode() impact HashMap/HashSet behavior, and what bugs can happen if they are implemented incorrectly?",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Hands-on Java development with code reviews and defect reduction work.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "In HashMap and HashSet, hashCode decides the bucket and equals confirms logical equality, so both must be consistent. If they are wrong, lookups and removals can fail and HashSet can keep duplicate logical objects. In practice, I review this carefully and prefer stable fields for equality so collection behavior stays predictable."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "What is the difference between checked and unchecked exceptions in Java, and how do you decide where to handle them?",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Backend API development and debugging in enterprise environments.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "I treat checked exceptions as recoverable cases like IO or database operations and handle or wrap them at clear boundaries. I use unchecked exceptions for programming and validation issues that should fail fast. In Spring APIs, I centralize exception handling so clients get consistent error responses and logs stay actionable for debugging."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "How does dependency injection in Spring improve testability and maintainability? Can you show how you used it?",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Spring Boot and unit testing experience with modular service design.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "Dependency injection reduces tight coupling because classes depend on interfaces instead of concrete implementations. I used constructor injection in Spring Boot services, which made unit tests easier with JUnit and Mockito and kept business logic isolated. It also improved maintainability because we could swap implementations without changing calling layers."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "How would you design a secure, versioned REST API in Spring Boot for a client-facing application?",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "REST API design, Spring Security, JWT, and API versioning at Morgan Stanley vendor projects.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "I would version endpoints from the start, for example with /api/v1, and keep contracts stable through validated request and response DTOs. For security, I would enforce authentication and authorization with Spring Security and JWT and apply least-privilege access by role. I used this pattern in enterprise modules with layered services and tests so changes stayed safe, scalable, and maintainable."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "Write or explain a SQL query with joins and aggregation to answer a real business question.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "PostgreSQL/MySQL experience and backend data-flow optimization work.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "A common pattern I use is finding top customers by order volume in a time window. I join customers and orders, filter by date, group by customer, then sort by COUNT of orders or SUM of order amount. I have used this style in PostgreSQL and MySQL for reporting and validation workflows where clarity and correctness matter."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "A query is running slowly in production; how would you diagnose and improve it?",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "situational",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Performance tuning and production support experience in enterprise applications.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "I start by reproducing the issue and checking the execution plan to identify whether the bottleneck is scans, joins, or indexing gaps. Then I tune query shape by tightening filters, selecting only needed columns, and validating index strategy against real predicates. Finally, I re-test with realistic data and monitor latency after deployment to confirm the improvement."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "What are the differences between JDBC, JPA, and Hibernate, and where does each fit in a Java/J2EE application?",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Hibernate/JPA experience plus Java backend engineering background.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "JDBC is low-level database access where I write SQL directly and map results manually. JPA is a standard API for ORM patterns, and Hibernate is a popular JPA implementation with practical ORM features. In my backend work I mostly used JPA and Hibernate for maintainable data access, and I use direct SQL-style approaches when I need tighter control."
[19:22:22] [backend]   },
[19:22:22] [backend]   {
[19:22:22] [backend]     "question": "What Maven lifecycle phases do you use most, and how do you handle dependency conflicts in a team codebase?",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Maven and Gradle usage with Jenkins CI pipelines and Git-based team workflows.",
[19:22:22] [backend]     "answer": "The phases I use most are clean, compile, test, and package, and we run them consistently in Jenkins pipelines. For dependency conflicts, I inspect the dependency tree, align versions in dependencyManagement, and exclude problematic transitive dependencies. That keeps builds reproducible and avoids module drift across teams."
