[21:01:40] [backend]     "answer": "JDBC is the low-level API where you write SQL and manage connections directly, JPA is the specification for ORM-style persistence, and Hibernate is a common JPA implementation. In my recent Spring Boot work, I have used JPA and Hibernate most because it speeds up development and keeps repository code cleaner. I use JDBC-style control when I need very explicit SQL behavior or fine-grained performance tuning, but my strongest hands-on depth is with JPA and Hibernate."
[21:01:40] [backend]   },
[21:01:40] [backend]   {
