[21:20:44] [backend]     "answer": "The OOP principles I use most are encapsulation, abstraction, and composition. In production, I applied them by separating controller, service, and repository responsibilities in Spring Boot, which made business logic easier to test and change. I also used interface-based design for cleaner contracts and reused modular frontend components in Angular and React with the same mindset."
[21:20:44] [backend]   },
[21:20:44] [backend]   {
[21:20:44] [backend]     "question": "When would you use List vs Set vs Map in Java, and what tradeoffs matter in real systems?",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Java development background and production API/service implementation.",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "answer": "I use List when order matters and duplicates are acceptable, Set when uniqueness matters, and Map when I need fast key-based lookups. In my current schema-matching research pipeline, Map is central for source-to-target field mappings, Set helps remove duplicate candidate mappings, and List is useful for ordered outputs in evaluation flows. The tradeoff is usually readability and correctness first, then performance based on access patterns."
[21:20:44] [backend]   },
[21:20:44] [backend]   {
[21:20:44] [backend]     "question": "How would you design a secure REST API in Spring Boot for a client-facing platform?",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Implemented API versioning and security with Spring Security and JWT.",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "answer": "I would start with a clear resource model, versioned endpoints, DTO validation, and consistent error handling so the API is predictable for clients. Then I’d secure it with Spring Security and JWT, enforce role-based authorization, and add logging for traceability of sensitive operations. In my Morgan Stanley work, this combination of API design plus security controls helped us keep services maintainable and safer in shared enterprise environments."
[21:20:44] [backend]   },
[21:20:44] [backend]   {
[21:20:44] [backend]     "question": "A service is slow due to database-heavy calls. How would you debug and optimize it?",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "type": "situational",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Improved API response performance and did production debugging/optimization.",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "answer": "I first reproduce the slowdown and isolate whether time is spent in SQL, service logic, or serialization using logs and profiling. Then I inspect query plans, indexing, join patterns, and potential N+1 access, and I optimize with better queries, batching, or pagination based on what the data shows. I’ve used this approach in Spring API optimization work and followed it with tests and monitoring to confirm the fix under realistic load."
[21:20:44] [backend]   },
[21:20:44] [backend]   {
[21:20:44] [backend]     "question": "Can you write or explain an SQL query with JOIN, GROUP BY, and filtering for a reporting requirement?",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "PostgreSQL/MySQL usage and backend data-flow work in Spring applications.",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "answer": "For a reporting use case, I’d typically join transactional and reference tables, filter by date or status, then group by a business dimension. For example: SELECT d.name, COUNT(t.id) FROM transactions t JOIN departments d ON t.dept_id = d.id WHERE t.status = 'SUCCESS' AND t.created_at >= ? GROUP BY d.name. That pattern is similar to how I’ve handled relational reporting logic with PostgreSQL and MySQL-backed services."
[21:20:44] [backend]   },
[21:20:44] [backend]   {
[21:20:44] [backend]     "question": "What is the difference between JDBC and JPA/Hibernate, and when would you choose each?",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "type": "technical",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "best_resume_match": "Experience with Hibernate/JPA and Java backend development.",
[21:20:44] [backend]     "answer": "My stronger hands-on experience is with JPA and Hibernate in Spring Boot, and I haven’t owned a pure JDBC-heavy module yet. JDBC gives low-level control over SQL and connections, which is useful for fine-tuned queries, while JPA/Hibernate improves productivity and maintainability through entity mapping and repository patterns. In practice, I start with JPA/Hibernate and use native SQL selectively when performance or query complexity requires it."
