[18:33:01] [backend] acquired in that period — not just ad spend. Most CAC calculations undercount costs. LTV calculation: for one-time
[18:33:01] [backend] healthcare purchases, average deal value plus referral multiplier plus repeat treatment rate. At Marketing Dollar: $12,000
[18:33:01] [backend] 
[18:33:01] [backend] initial + ($12,000 x 30% repeat rate) + ($12,000 x 10% referral rate) = $16,800 LTV. For subscription: monthly price
[18:33:01] [backend] multiplied by average subscription length. At Stars Wellness: $44 per month x 8-month average = $352 LTV.
[18:33:01] [backend] How the ratio drives optimization. If LTV/CAC is above 5x, I know I have room to scale aggressively — the unit
[18:33:01] [backend] economics
[18:33:01] [backend] absorb higher CPAs as audiences expand. If LTV/CAC drops below 3x, I'm in efficiency mode — cut anything not hitting
[18:33:01] [backend] target CPA. Payback period determines aggression: at Stars Wellness, 2-week payback meant I could spend confidently
[18:33:01] [backend] knowing recovery was fast. At Ardent, a longer payback on a $180 CPA against a $12K LTV still justified aggressive
[18:33:01] [backend] scaling
[18:33:01] [backend] because the lifetime return was exceptional.
[18:33:01] [backend] Cohort analysis monthly: track LTV by acquisition month to see if the quality of customers is improving or declining. If
[18:33:01] [backend] LTV
[18:33:01] [backend] is rising, acquisition efficiency is improving even if CPA is flat. I'd implement this tracking at We Inspect in the first 30
