[18:33:01] [backend] Q104. A CMO wants to cut paid media budget 30% next quarter. How do you respond?
[18:33:01] [backend] COACH: Model first, opinion second. You become a strategic partner, not an obstacle.
[18:33:01] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person protects the business with data, not with ego."
[18:33:01] [backend] I respond with a scenario model before I respond with an opinion — because if I defend the budget emotionally, I become
[18:33:01] [backend] an
[18:33:01] [backend] obstacle. If I come with a financial model, I become a strategic partner. That's the only frame I ever use for budget
[18:33:01] [backend] conversations.
[18:33:01] [backend] Step one before the meeting: build a scenario analysis. Current spend, current revenue, current ROAS. Then 30% reduced
[18:33:01] [backend] spend, projected revenue impact. Then the net position — what does the company lose in pipeline vs what it saves in media
[18:33:01] [backend] spend? For well-optimized campaigns, a 30% budget cut typically produces a greater than 30% revenue reduction, because
[18:33:01] [backend] you cut into the most efficient spend segments first. I make that math visible before the decision is made.
[18:33:01] [backend] Step two: audit for real inefficiency first. In almost every account I've managed, there's 10-15% of spend in
[18:33:01] [backend] underperforming
[18:33:01] [backend] ad groups that can be cut without touching performance. "Before we reduce the budget, let me find you $X in savings that
[18:33:01] [backend] won't cost you revenue." That reframes the conversation from defending my budget to partnering on their P&L problem.
[18:33:01] [backend] Step three, if the cut is unavoidable: protect retargeting and conversion-stage spend — highest direct ROAS, clearest
[18:33:01] [backend] revenue
[18:33:01] [backend] attribution. Cut upper-funnel proportionally. Document the expected pipeline impact so the decision is made with full
[18:33:01] [backend] information.
