[18:33:01] [backend] Meta healthcare targeting restrictions are real — I've tracked these policy changes specifically because they were a live
[18:33:01] [backend] operational risk in my work. The platform has progressively tightened rules around fertility, mental health, and chronic
[18:33:01] [backend] condition advertising.
[18:33:01] [backend] First 48 hours: diagnose before acting. Is it targeting parameters that were removed, or is it ad delivery restriction triggered
[18:33:01] [backend] by landing page content? Completely different problems. I check the Policy Center directly, run a test ad to understand the
[18:33:01] [backend] restriction type, and look at which specific audiences or interest categories were affected.
[18:33:01] [backend] If targeting parameters were removed: I pivot to signals that remain available. Custom audiences from website visitors who
[18:33:01] [backend] viewed IVF-specific pages — highest-intent people I already have. Lookalike audiences from existing lead lists. Interest
[18:33:01] [backend] targeting in adjacent categories — parenting planning, reproductive health adjacent topics — that aren't restricted. Less
[18:33:01] [backend] precise, but live within hours.
[18:33:01] [backend] If the landing page is triggering the restriction: I restructure the creative and landing page to remove the specific health
[18:33:01] [backend] claim
[18:33:01] [backend] language Meta flags while preserving the message's intent. Compliance reframing is a real skill — and faster than channel
[18:33:01] [backend] migration.
[18:33:01] [backend] Medium-term: accelerate Google Search investment (captures existing demand, less restrictive for healthcare), Pinterest and
[18:33:01] [backend] YouTube for awareness, email campaigns to the existing lead list.
[18:33:01] [backend] The longer-term lesson I'd state directly to any stakeholder: this is why I never allow 100% single-platform dependency. At
[18:33:01] [backend] Marketing Dollar, single-channel concentration was my biggest operational risk. I always recommend a documented
[18:33:01] [backend] twochannel minimum with contingency plans for both. I'd build that redundancy at We Inspect before we ever face this
[18:33:01] [backend] scenario.
