[18:54:05] [backend] A 30-day consideration cycle is not a reason to fly blind — it's a reason to build a richer measurement stack than most
[18:54:05] [backend] advertisers use. Here's how I approach it.
[18:54:05] [backend] I build four levels of conversion tracking — each serving a different purpose.
[18:54:05] [backend] Level one — engagement signals: time on site above 2 minutes, specific page views (process pages, pricing pages), video
[18:54:05] [backend] completions above 50%. These are the earliest intent indicators — too soft to optimize against, but valuable as audience
[18:54:05] [backend] signals for retargeting.
[18:54:05] [backend] Level two — intent signals: lead form opens, partial form completions, click-to-call events. These indicate someone is
[18:54:05] [backend] evaluating seriously. I import these into Google Ads as micro-conversions to feed smart bidding data during the learning
[18:54:05] [backend] period when final conversions are sparse.
[18:54:05] [backend] Level three — qualified actions: completed form submissions, consultation request confirmations. These become the
[18:54:05] [backend] primary
[18:54:05] [backend] optimization target for both Google and Meta campaign bidding strategies. This is where I direct the algorithm.
[18:54:05] [backend] Level four — business outcomes: booked consultations, appointments kept, closed deals. These live in Salesforce CRM and
[18:54:05] [backend] 
[18:54:05] [backend] get connected back to campaign data via UTM parameters. This is the layer that produces true revenue ROAS — not
[18:54:05] [backend] leadbased ROAS, which is often meaningfully different.
