[19:11:23] [backend] Q63. How do you handle attribution in multi-touch customer journeys?
[19:11:23] [backend] COACH: Show the platform bias problem and GA4 as source of truth. Reference the Ardent assisted conversion example.
[19:11:23] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person would not lose our Meta budget because of a last-click reporting problem."
[19:11:23] [backend] At Ardent, proving Instagram's assisted conversion value — using multi-touch attribution rather than last-click — saved the
[19:11:23] [backend] Meta budget from being cut. Without that analysis, we would have eliminated a channel responsible for 30% of
[19:11:23] [backend] consultation
[19:11:23] [backend] pipeline because last-click gave it zero credit.
[19:11:23] [backend] The platform bias problem: both Google and Meta claim credit for the same conversion. Last-click attribution gives 100%
[19:11:23] [backend] credit to the final touchpoint. In a journey where the user saw a Meta awareness ad, searched Google three days later, and
[19:11:23] [backend] converted from Search — last-click gives Google 100% and Meta zero. The actual contribution was shared. Most budget
[19:11:23] [backend] allocation decisions made on last-click data systematically undervalue upper-funnel channels by 40-60%.
[19:11:23] [backend] My approach: data-driven or position-based attribution in GA4 as the primary measurement tool — 40% to first touch, 40%
[19:11:23] [backend] to last, 20% to middle touchpoints. More honest than last-click, and it surfaces the channels doing demand creation work
[19:11:23] [backend] that
[19:11:23] [backend] never gets credit.
[19:11:23] [backend] For cross-platform journeys: UTM parameters on every paid ad, tracked through GA4. At Ardent, I tracked "assisted
[19:11:23] [backend] conversions" — channels that appear in the path but aren't last click. Instagram had low last-click conversions but a high
[19:11:23] [backend] assist rate. That's the evidence that saved the budget.
[19:11:23] [backend] For multi-device journeys: GA4's cross-device reporting showed 35% of conversions at Marketing Dollar involved multiple
[19:11:23] [backend] devices. Without tracking this, mobile's awareness contribution was invisible.
[19:11:23] [backend] I'd set up multi-touch attribution infrastructure at We Inspect before the first demand campaign launches.
[19:11:23] [backend] Q64. How do you measure incrementality — proving ads drive new customers vs capturing existing
[19:11:23] [backend] demand?
[19:11:23] [backend] COACH: Show geo holdout and time-based holdout methods. Reference Stars Wellness 88% lift result.
[19:11:23] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person knows the difference between correlation and causation in marketing data."
[19:11:23] [backend] At Stars Wellness, a geo holdout test proved 88% of conversions were ad-driven — not organic demand. That single test
[19:11:23] [backend] justified scaling the budget from $2K to $6K in January, generating 450 conversions at $13.33 CPA. The incrementality
[19:11:23] [backend] test
[19:11:23] [backend] was the business case.
[19:11:23] [backend] The core problem: conversions happening while ads run doesn't mean ads caused them. People who were going to convert
[19:11:23] [backend] anyway inflate your reported ROAS. If you measure lift incorrectly, you overpay for demand you already had.
[19:11:23] [backend] Five methods I've used. Geo holdout: turn off ads in specific cities, keep ads running everywhere else, compare conversion
[19:11:23] [backend] rates. At Stars Wellness, test cities generated 100 conversions vs 12 in control cities — proving 88% incremental lift.
[19:11:23] [backend] Timebased holdout: turn off all ads for one to two weeks and measure conversion drop. At Ardent, pausing Google Ads for
[19:11:23] [backend] one
[19:11:23] [backend] week dropped weekly consultations from 12 to 4 — proving 67% of consultations were ad-driven. Brand search analysis: if
[19:11:23] [backend] campaigns drive awareness, branded search volume increases. At M.A.S.S., launching Instagram campaigns produced a
[19:11:23] [backend] 60%
[19:11:23] [backend] increase in branded Google searches — proving awareness lift. New vs returning customer split: at Marketing Dollar, 95%
[19:11:23] [backend] of
[19:11:23] [backend] 
[19:11:23] [backend] ad-driven conversions were first-time patients — direct incremental proof. Attribution window analysis: if conversions
[19:11:23] [backend] happen immediately after click, ads may be capturing existing demand; if they take 7-14 days, ads are influencing a
[19:11:23] [backend] decision.
[19:11:23] [backend] I'd design and run an incrementality test at We Inspect within the first 90 days. The result should drive budget allocation
[19:11:23] [backend] decisions, not
[19:11:23] [backend] assumptions.
