[19:11:23] [backend] Q104. A CMO wants to cut paid media budget 30% next quarter. How do you respond?
[19:11:23] [backend] COACH: Model first, opinion second. You become a strategic partner, not an obstacle.
[19:11:23] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person protects the business with data, not with ego."
[19:11:23] [backend] I respond with a scenario model before I respond with an opinion — because if I defend the budget emotionally, I become
[19:11:23] [backend] an
[19:11:23] [backend] obstacle. If I come with a financial model, I become a strategic partner. That's the only frame I ever use for budget
[19:11:23] [backend] conversations.
[19:11:23] [backend] Step one before the meeting: build a scenario analysis. Current spend, current revenue, current ROAS. Then 30% reduced
[19:11:23] [backend] spend, projected revenue impact. Then the net position — what does the company lose in pipeline vs what it saves in media
[19:11:23] [backend] spend? For well-optimized campaigns, a 30% budget cut typically produces a greater than 30% revenue reduction, because
[19:11:23] [backend] you cut into the most efficient spend segments first. I make that math visible before the decision is made.
[19:11:23] [backend] Step two: audit for real inefficiency first. In almost every account I've managed, there's 10-15% of spend in
[19:11:23] [backend] underperforming
[19:11:23] [backend] ad groups that can be cut without touching performance. "Before we reduce the budget, let me find you $X in savings that
[19:11:23] [backend] won't cost you revenue." That reframes the conversation from defending my budget to partnering on their P&L problem.
[19:11:23] [backend] Step three, if the cut is unavoidable: protect retargeting and conversion-stage spend — highest direct ROAS, clearest
[19:11:23] [backend] revenue
[19:11:23] [backend] attribution. Cut upper-funnel proportionally. Document the expected pipeline impact so the decision is made with full
[19:11:23] [backend] information.
[19:11:23] [backend] At Ardent, a version of this conversation about Meta vs Google allocation ended in a reallocation, not a cut — because I
[19:11:23] [backend] came with attribution data showing incremental contribution by channel. Performance held. That's how I protect the
[19:11:23] [backend] business
[19:11:23] [backend] from decisions made on assumptions. I'd bring the same rigor to We Inspect.
