[19:14:34] [backend] 23% once the primary audience saturates, geographic expansion one city or region at a time with distinct performance
[19:14:34] [backend] benchmarking per location, placement expansion if feed performs well then test stories. Each expansion treated as a
[19:14:34] [backend] separate
[19:14:34] [backend] test with its own performance evaluation window.
[19:14:34] [backend] Vertical scaling — increase budget: 20% increments, monitoring CPA during each phase. If CPA increases more than 15%,
[19:14:34] [backend] pause the scale and optimize before continuing. At Marketing Dollar, I hit the $1,200/day ceiling when CPA jumped from
[19:14:34] [backend] $375 to $510 — stopped scaling, focused on efficiency improvements, brought CPA back to $400 before resuming.
[19:14:34] [backend] Creative scaling in parallel: you cannot scale budget without scaling creative variety. At Marketing Dollar, I produced new
[19:14:34] [backend] creative every two weeks during the scaling phase and ran 8-10 active creative variations simultaneously to prevent
[19:14:34] [backend] frequency fatigue.
[19:14:34] [backend] When to stop: CPA increases more than 20% and doesn't stabilize, conversion rate drops more than 15%, audience becomes
[19:14:34] [backend] too broad and quality declines. The data tells you where the ceiling is — the discipline is in listening to it.
[19:14:34] [backend] That systematic approach is what I'd bring to scaling at We Inspect.
