[19:14:34] [backend] Q79. How do you prove ROI of top-of-funnel spend to a skeptical stakeholder?
[19:14:34] [backend] COACH: Show cohort analysis and holdout testing as your weapons. Translate everything into dollar terms before the meeting.
[19:14:34] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person can protect the right budget decisions with evidence, not just arguments."
[19:14:34] [backend] This is one of the most commercially important skills in paid media management — and where most marketers fail their
[19:14:34] [backend] stakeholders by showing them last-click numbers and calling it proof.
[19:14:34] [backend] The most credible approach I've developed: cohort analysis paired with holdout testing.
[19:14:34] [backend] Cohort analysis: I track leads by first-touch channel and follow their conversion journey. At Ardent, I could show that leads
[19:14:34] [backend] who first engaged with a Meta awareness ad — before they ever searched on Google — had measurably higher consultation
[19:14:34] [backend] booking rates than cold Google Search leads. That's direct evidence that upper-funnel spend was improving lower-funnel
[19:14:34] [backend] quality, not just adding cost. The mechanism was visible in the data.
[19:14:34] [backend] Holdout testing: suppress awareness campaigns for a defined segment for two weeks and watch whether Search volume
[19:14:34] [backend] holds. It typically drops — and that drop quantifies the incremental contribution of upper-funnel spend. The evidence is
[19:14:34] [backend] now
[19:14:34] [backend] in the language a CFO trusts: "Cutting Meta would have reduced Google-attributed consultations by approximately X, at a
[19:14:34] [backend] revenue cost of $Y."
[19:14:34] [backend] For any stakeholder conversation about top-of-funnel: I translate everything into dollar terms before walking into the room.
[19:14:34] [backend] Not "CPM was $8" — "for every $1,000 in awareness spend, we drove $X in down-funnel pipeline value." That's the frame
[19:14:34] [backend] executives can evaluate against their priorities.
