[19:14:34] [backend] I build four levels of conversion tracking — each serving a different purpose.
[19:14:34] [backend] Level one — engagement signals: time on site above 2 minutes, specific page views (process pages, pricing pages), video
[19:14:34] [backend] completions above 50%. These are the earliest intent indicators — too soft to optimize against, but valuable as audience
[19:14:34] [backend] signals for retargeting.
[19:14:34] [backend] Level two — intent signals: lead form opens, partial form completions, click-to-call events. These indicate someone is
[19:14:34] [backend] evaluating seriously. I import these into Google Ads as micro-conversions to feed smart bidding data during the learning
[19:14:34] [backend] period when final conversions are sparse.
[19:14:34] [backend] Level three — qualified actions: completed form submissions, consultation request confirmations. These become the
[19:14:34] [backend] primary
[19:14:34] [backend] optimization target for both Google and Meta campaign bidding strategies. This is where I direct the algorithm.
[19:14:34] [backend] Level four — business outcomes: booked consultations, appointments kept, closed deals. These live in Salesforce CRM and
[19:14:34] [backend] 
[19:14:34] [backend] get connected back to campaign data via UTM parameters. This is the layer that produces true revenue ROAS — not
[19:14:34] [backend] leadbased ROAS, which is often meaningfully different.
[19:14:34] [backend] At Ardent, this four-level stack connected to Tableau gave us campaign-to-revenue visibility that most healthcare
[19:14:34] [backend] advertisers
[19:14:34] [backend] don't have. The result was decisions made on cost-per-closed-consultation, not cost-per-lead. That's a fundamentally
[19:14:34] [backend] different
[19:14:34] [backend] optimization target — and it's why we hit the business metric that actually mattered. I'd build this stack here within the first
[19:14:34] [backend] two weeks.
[19:14:34] [backend] Q81. You have GA4, Tableau, and Salesforce. How do you connect them into one performance view?
