[19:14:34] [backend] Q112. You inherit a $100K/month underperforming paid media account. First 30 days.
[19:14:34] [backend] COACH: Restraint in week one is itself a signal of seniority.
[19:14:34] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person would not blow up our account trying to prove themselves in week one."
[19:14:34] [backend] First principle: the most expensive thing I can do in week one is make changes. Most underperforming accounts have a
[19:14:34] [backend] history of reactive changes that compounded the problem. I need to understand that history before I add to it.
[19:14:34] [backend] Week one — full audit, zero changes: build a complete performance map showing ROAS, CPL, conversion rate, and trend
[19:14:34] [backend] direction for every campaign over the last 90 days. Pull the change history log and read it forensically — what was
[19:14:34] [backend] changed,
[19:14:34] [backend] when, and what happened to performance afterward. Also talk to the sales team. Underperformance at the conversion stage
[19:14:34] [backend] is
[19:14:34] [backend] often a handoff problem, not a media problem — if I optimize the ads without understanding the lead quality issue
[19:14:34] [backend] downstream, I'm solving the wrong problem.
[19:14:34] [backend] 
[19:14:34] [backend] Week two — identify the single highest-leverage fix: not five things. One. Typically: wasted spend on irrelevant search
[19:14:34] [backend] terms, a broken qualification funnel passing unqualified leads downstream, or a budget allocation mismatch where the most
[19:14:34] [backend] efficient campaigns are budget-capped while inefficient ones run freely. I fix that one thing, document the hypothesis
[19:14:34] [backend] explicitly, and set a two-week minimum evaluation window.
[19:14:34] [backend] Weeks three and four — structural improvements: proper match type architecture, comprehensive negative keyword lists,
[19:14:34] [backend] audience segmentation. These are right to do but don't produce immediate results — I sequence them after the
[19:14:34] [backend] highestleverage fix so there's early momentum to point to.
[19:14:34] [backend] End of 30 days — I present: what I found, what I changed, what I expect in the next 60 days, and what I need from the
[19:14:34] [backend] stakeholder team. The inputs matter as much as the plan.
[19:14:34] [backend] Discipline in week one is how you earn trust for the next eleven months.
[19:14:34] [backend] Q113. Meta restricts healthcare targeting for your IVF clinic. Their main channel goes dark. What do
[19:14:34] [backend] you do?
[19:14:34] [backend] COACH: Diagnose restriction type first. Show contingency channels.
[19:14:34] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person would not panic, and we would not lose our pipeline for a month."
[19:14:34] [backend] Meta healthcare targeting restrictions are real — I've tracked these policy changes specifically because they were a live
[19:14:34] [backend] operational risk in my work. The platform has progressively tightened rules around fertility, mental health, and chronic
[19:14:34] [backend] condition advertising.
[19:14:34] [backend] First 48 hours: diagnose before acting. Is it targeting parameters that were removed, or is it ad delivery restriction triggered
[19:14:34] [backend] by landing page content? Completely different problems. I check the Policy Center directly, run a test ad to understand the
[19:14:34] [backend] restriction type, and look at which specific audiences or interest categories were affected.
[19:14:34] [backend] If targeting parameters were removed: I pivot to signals that remain available. Custom audiences from website visitors who
[19:14:34] [backend] viewed IVF-specific pages — highest-intent people I already have. Lookalike audiences from existing lead lists. Interest
[19:14:34] [backend] targeting in adjacent categories — parenting planning, reproductive health adjacent topics — that aren't restricted. Less
[19:14:34] [backend] precise, but live within hours.
[19:14:34] [backend] If the landing page is triggering the restriction: I restructure the creative and landing page to remove the specific health
[19:14:34] [backend] claim
[19:14:34] [backend] language Meta flags while preserving the message's intent. Compliance reframing is a real skill — and faster than channel
[19:14:34] [backend] migration.
[19:14:34] [backend] Medium-term: accelerate Google Search investment (captures existing demand, less restrictive for healthcare), Pinterest and
[19:14:34] [backend] YouTube for awareness, email campaigns to the existing lead list.
[19:14:34] [backend] The longer-term lesson I'd state directly to any stakeholder: this is why I never allow 100% single-platform dependency. At
[19:14:34] [backend] Marketing Dollar, single-channel concentration was my biggest operational risk. I always recommend a documented
[19:14:34] [backend] twochannel minimum with contingency plans for both. I'd build that redundancy at We Inspect before we ever face this
[19:14:34] [backend] scenario.
