[19:14:34] [backend] impressions.'"
[19:14:34] [backend] Before I allocate a dollar, I need one number: the LTV-to-CAC ratio that makes this business model viable. If LTV is $200
[19:14:34] [backend] and the target payback period is 12 months, my CAC ceiling is roughly $50-60. That determines channel eligibility — not
[19:14:34] [backend] every channel can generate subscriptions at that cost.
[19:14:34] [backend] 
[19:14:34] [backend] With $50K/month and a typical subscription LTV: $28K to Google Search — 56%. Captures existing demand,
[19:14:34] [backend] intentqualified, clearest path to trial or paid signup. Branded terms protected, non-brand organized by problem-solution
[19:14:34] [backend] intent.
[19:14:34] [backend] $15K to Meta — 30%. Demand creation and retargeting. Cold prospecting via lookalikes built from paying subscribers —
[19:14:34] [backend] not trial users. Retargeting of website visitors and trial users who haven't converted, with distinct creative for each
[19:14:34] [backend] audience's
[19:14:34] [backend] specific friction point. $5K to YouTube pre-roll — 10%. Subscription products benefit from demonstration. A 30-second
[19:14:34] [backend] "product in use" video builds consideration that Search then captures. $2K held for new channel testing — 4%. One
[19:14:34] [backend] structured test per quarter.
[19:14:34] [backend] Launch sequence: weeks one through three — conversion tracking stack, UTM infrastructure, Tableau dashboard live
[19:14:34] [backend] before
[19:14:34] [backend] a dollar is spent. Weeks four through eight — learning phase, minimum budgets for smart bidding signal accumulation.
[19:14:34] [backend] Month three — full optimization, lookalike expansion from paying subscriber base, first channel test.
[19:14:34] [backend] 90-day review: actual CAC by channel vs target, allocation adjusted based on real data. The plan is a starting hypothesis.
[19:14:34] [backend] The
[19:14:34] [backend] data is the truth. I'd never be locked into the initial allocation if the first 90 days prove it wrong.
[19:14:34] [backend] Q115. Boss says: 3x more leads next quarter, same budget. How do you respond?
[19:14:34] [backend] COACH: Honest before strategic. Show the audit framework for finding real improvement.
[19:14:34] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person will tell us the truth — and that honesty will protect us from bad targets."
[19:14:34] [backend] I respond honestly before I respond strategically — because promising 3x and delivering 1.5x is worse than setting a
[19:14:34] [backend] realistic
[19:14:34] [backend] expectation at the start.
[19:14:34] [backend] My first response: "Three times more leads means reducing CPL by 67%. That's achievable in some specific scenarios, but
[19:14:34] [backend] I
[19:14:34] [backend] want to make sure we're aligned on what's realistic before I commit to a number." That's not pushback — that's protecting
[19:14:34] [backend] the
[19:14:34] [backend] business from a target set on aspiration rather than evidence.
[19:14:34] [backend] Then I do a rapid audit of where genuine CPL improvement can actually come from. Landing page conversion rate first: if
[19:14:34] [backend] we're converting at 2% and the category average is 5-7%, there's a genuine 2-3x improvement available through CRO alone
[19:14:34] [backend] — without spending more. This is the highest-leverage lever if the gap exists. Audience expansion second: if current
[19:14:34] [backend] targeting is narrow and fully saturated, lookalike expansion from quality converters can unlock volume at comparable CPL.
[19:14:34] [backend] Creative refresh third: if we've been running the same creative for 6-plus months, frequency fatigue is artificially inflating
[19:14:34] [backend] CPL. A significant refresh has historically produced 20-40% CPL improvement in mature accounts.
[19:14:34] [backend] If none of those paths credibly reach 3x: I'd say exactly that. "We can improve meaningfully — I'd estimate 40-60% CPL
[19:14:34] [backend] reduction is achievable within 90 days through these three levers. Getting to 3x would require either additional budget, a
[19:14:34] [backend] fundamentally different channel mix, or accepting lower lead quality to hit volume. Which of those is the actual priority?"
[19:14:34] [backend] 3x improvement on a well-optimized account is a budget conversation or a quality conversation. I'd rather have that
[19:14:34] [backend] conversation now than face it during a quarterly review.
[19:14:34] [backend] Q116. The product you're marketing has bad reviews online. How does that affect your strategy?
[19:14:34] [backend] COACH: Show strategic thinking. Show willingness to have the hard conversation.
[19:14:34] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person would tell us the truth about a product problem instead of spending into it."
[19:14:34] [backend] Bad reviews change the paid media strategy fundamentally — and pretending otherwise is how companies burn budget
[19:14:34] [backend] accelerating churn.
[19:14:34] [backend] 
[19:14:34] [backend] First: I diagnose the nature and distribution of the reviews. Are they about a specific resolved issue, or an ongoing product
[19:14:34] [backend] failure? Are they concentrated in a specific use case or customer segment? That distinction determines what's fixable with
[19:14:34] [backend] marketing and what requires product work first.
[19:14:34] [backend] If the product has a genuine unresolved issue: I'd be direct with the stakeholder. "Sending more paid traffic to a product
[19:14:34] [backend] with
[19:14:34] [backend] a 2-star average will produce more churn, more negative reviews, and more wasted spend. Every dollar we invest brings in
[19:14:34] [backend] a
[19:14:34] [backend] customer who will amplify the negative signal when they leave. We need to either fix the product issue first or narrow our
[19:14:34] [backend] targeting to the specific use case where reviews are positive." That's a hard conversation. It's also the most important one —
[19:14:34] [backend] and avoiding it is how performance marketers destroy trust by delivering short-term traffic against a long-term brand
[19:14:34] [backend] problem.
[19:14:34] [backend] If the reviews are segment-specific: I narrow targeting aggressively to the customer profile where satisfaction is
[19:14:34] [backend] demonstrably high. Same product, different audience, meaningfully different outcome.
[19:14:34] [backend] If the reviews reflect a past issue that has been genuinely resolved: I address it proactively in ad creative. Specific copy
[19:14:34] [backend] acknowledging what changed, with proof, can turn a review liability into a credibility asset. Transparency about past
[19:14:34] [backend] problems paired with genuine improvement builds trust faster than polished brand messaging.
[19:14:34] [backend] At Stars Wellness, we were entering a market with a stronger-branded competitor. Research-first positioning based on
[19:14:34] [backend] 10,000
[19:14:34] [backend] surveys found the exact segment that needed what we offered. The result was 125% ROI vs a competitor losing $5 per
[19:14:34] [backend] acquisition. Targeting the right audience honestly beats targeting broadly with a compromised product every time.
[19:14:34] [backend] Q117. What do you do when a platform algorithm update kills your best-performing campaign
[19:14:34] [backend] overnight?
[19:14:34] [backend] COACH: Verify first. Systematic response. Protect pipeline during testing.
[19:14:34] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person would not panic and cost us budget through reactive changes."
[19:14:34] [backend] The first thing I do is verify it's actually a platform change — because most "algorithm update" crises I've encountered
[19:14:34] [backend] turned
[19:14:34] [backend] out to be something else.
[19:14:34] [backend] Bid strategy auto-adjustments the platform made quietly. A retargeting audience that hit saturation. Creative burnout after
[19:14:34] [backend] heavy frequency. Seasonal intent shifts. All of these feel like algorithm changes when you're looking at a sudden
[19:14:34] [backend] performance
[19:14:34] [backend] drop. Most of them aren't.
[19:14:34] [backend] Verification process: check the official Google Ads and Meta changelogs first — not industry Twitter, the actual release
[19:14:34] [backend] notes. Check the Google Ads community and Meta Ads Manager community where practitioners in the same vertical share
[19:14:34] [backend] real-time data. If the drop is systemic, multiple advertisers in my vertical will be reporting it simultaneously.
[19:14:34] [backend] If confirmed as a platform change: pull performance data for the last seven days vs the same window four weeks prior to
[19:14:34] [backend] isolate the drop from seasonal noise. Identify which metric deteriorated first — impressions, CTR, or conversion rate —
[19:14:34] [backend] because that tells me exactly where in the system the change hit.
[19:14:34] [backend] My response: do not make wholesale account changes. Run a controlled test — one campaign adapted to the new platform
[19:14:34] [backend] mechanics, one holding the previous structure — with a two-week evaluation window. Data tells me whether the adaptation
[19:14:34] [backend] is necessary and what form it should take.
[19:14:34] [backend] During the testing period: shift budget toward the channel that's holding performance to protect lead volume for the
[19:14:34] [backend] business.
[19:14:34] [backend] The business doesn't pause while I diagnose.
[19:14:34] [backend] 
[19:14:34] [backend] Composure and process — not speed and guesswork. That's what I'd bring to We Inspect.
