[20:00:53] [backend] First: I diagnose the nature and distribution of the reviews. Are they about a specific resolved issue, or an ongoing product
[20:00:53] [backend] failure? Are they concentrated in a specific use case or customer segment? That distinction determines what's fixable with
[20:00:53] [backend] marketing and what requires product work first.
[20:00:53] [backend] If the product has a genuine unresolved issue: I'd be direct with the stakeholder. "Sending more paid traffic to a product
[20:00:53] [backend] with
[20:00:53] [backend] a 2-star average will produce more churn, more negative reviews, and more wasted spend. Every dollar we invest brings in
[20:00:53] [backend] a
[20:00:53] [backend] customer who will amplify the negative signal when they leave. We need to either fix the product issue first or narrow our
[20:00:53] [backend] targeting to the specific use case where reviews are positive." That's a hard conversation. It's also the most important one —
[20:00:53] [backend] and avoiding it is how performance marketers destroy trust by delivering short-term traffic against a long-term brand
[20:00:53] [backend] problem.
[20:00:53] [backend] If the reviews are segment-specific: I narrow targeting aggressively to the customer profile where satisfaction is
[20:00:53] [backend] demonstrably high. Same product, different audience, meaningfully different outcome.
[20:00:53] [backend] If the reviews reflect a past issue that has been genuinely resolved: I address it proactively in ad creative. Specific copy
[20:00:53] [backend] acknowledging what changed, with proof, can turn a review liability into a credibility asset. Transparency about past
[20:00:53] [backend] problems paired with genuine improvement builds trust faster than polished brand messaging.
[20:00:53] [backend] At Stars Wellness, we were entering a market with a stronger-branded competitor. Research-first positioning based on
[20:00:53] [backend] 10,000
[20:00:53] [backend] surveys found the exact segment that needed what we offered. The result was 125% ROI vs a competitor losing $5 per
[20:00:53] [backend] acquisition. Targeting the right audience honestly beats targeting broadly with a compromised product every time.
