[20:00:53] [backend] stakeholders by showing them last-click numbers and calling it proof.
[20:00:53] [backend] The most credible approach I've developed: cohort analysis paired with holdout testing.
[20:00:53] [backend] Cohort analysis: I track leads by first-touch channel and follow their conversion journey. At Ardent, I could show that leads
[20:00:53] [backend] who first engaged with a Meta awareness ad — before they ever searched on Google — had measurably higher consultation
[20:00:53] [backend] booking rates than cold Google Search leads. That's direct evidence that upper-funnel spend was improving lower-funnel
[20:00:53] [backend] quality, not just adding cost. The mechanism was visible in the data.
[20:00:53] [backend] Holdout testing: suppress awareness campaigns for a defined segment for two weeks and watch whether Search volume
[20:00:53] [backend] holds. It typically drops — and that drop quantifies the incremental contribution of upper-funnel spend. The evidence is
[20:00:53] [backend] now
[20:00:53] [backend] in the language a CFO trusts: "Cutting Meta would have reduced Google-attributed consultations by approximately X, at a
[20:00:53] [backend] revenue cost of $Y."
