[20:00:53] [backend] Q119. A platform algorithm update kills your best-performing campaign overnight. What do you do?
[20:00:53] [backend] COACH: Show composure and systematic response. Panic changes are the most expensive mistake. Verify before acting.
[20:00:53] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person would not cost us budget through a reactive overreaction to a misread"
[20:00:53] [backend] situation.'"
[20:00:53] [backend] The first thing I do is verify that it's actually a platform change — because most "algorithm update" crises I've encountered
[20:00:53] [backend] in
[20:00:53] [backend] practice turned out to be something else entirely.
[20:00:53] [backend] Bid strategy auto-adjustments the platform made quietly. A retargeting audience that hit saturation. Creative burnout after
[20:00:53] [backend] 21
[20:00:53] [backend] days of heavy frequency. Seasonal intent shifts. All of these feel like platform changes when you're looking at a sudden
[20:00:53] [backend] performance drop. Most of them aren't.
[20:00:53] [backend] My verification process: I check the official Google Ads and Meta changelogs first — not industry Twitter, not marketing
[20:00:53] [backend] podcasts, the actual release notes. I look at the Google Ads community and the Meta Ads Manager community where
[20:00:53] [backend] 
[20:00:53] [backend] practitioners in the same vertical share real-time data. If the drop is systemic, multiple advertisers will be reporting it
[20:00:53] [backend] simultaneously.
[20:00:53] [backend] If it is confirmed as a platform change: I pull the last seven days of performance data versus the same window four weeks
[20:00:53] [backend] prior to isolate the drop from seasonal noise. Then I identify which metric deteriorated first — impressions, CTR, or
[20:00:53] [backend] conversion rate — because that tells me where in the system the change hit and what lever to pull.
[20:00:53] [backend] My response: I do not make wholesale account changes. I run a controlled test — one campaign adapted to the new
[20:00:53] [backend] platform
[20:00:53] [backend] mechanics, one campaign holding the previous structure — with a two-week evaluation window. Data tells me whether the
[20:00:53] [backend] adaptation is necessary and what form it should take.
[20:00:53] [backend] During the testing period: I shift budget toward the channel that's holding performance to protect lead volume for the
[20:00:53] [backend] business. The business doesn't pause while I diagnose.
[20:00:53] [backend] This approach — verify, diagnose precisely, test before changing broadly, protect pipeline during testing — is how I'd
[20:00:53] [backend] handle
[20:00:53] [backend] any platform disruption here. Composure and process, not speed and guesswork.
[20:00:53] [backend] Q120. How do you balance short-term performance goals with long-term growth?
[20:00:53] [backend] COACH: Portfolio approach. 80/20 split. Marketing Dollar compounding growth.
[20:00:53] [backend] Leave them thinking: "Short-term pays the bills. Long-term builds the future. Need both."
[20:00:53] [backend] At Marketing Dollar, I grew from one winning campaign to five over six months while maintaining 28x-32x ROAS. The
[20:00:53] [backend] mechanism: 80% of budget drove this month's results while 20% tested new audiences and creative. Tests graduated into
[20:00:53] [backend] the
[20:00:53] [backend] proven portfolio monthly, compounding growth.
[20:00:53] [backend] Balance shifts by context. End of quarter at risk: shift to 85/15. Plateauing performance: shift to 60/40. I present both
[20:00:53] [backend] stories:
[20:00:53] [backend] "This month: 61 conversions at $180 CPA" and "Future investments: video ads at $165 CPA, graduating to main budget."
[20:00:53] [backend] Short-term pays the bills. Long-term builds the future. I'd establish that dual-track approach at We Inspect with clear
[20:00:53] [backend] graduation
[20:00:53] [backend] criteria.
[20:00:53] [backend] Q121. How do you think about brand awareness versus performance marketing?
[20:00:53] [backend] COACH: Performance-first. Marketing Dollar incrementality test. Brand as natural side effect.
[20:00:53] [backend] Leave them thinking: "Performance marketing first — brand builds as a side effect of doing it well."
[20:00:53] [backend] At Marketing Dollar, I tested whether campaigns were building brand or just capturing demand by pausing all ads for one
[20:00:53] [backend] week. Bookings dropped 70%, proving performance campaigns were the primary demand driver.
[20:00:53] [backend] My philosophy: performance first at 80% focus and budget, brand as force multiplier at 20%. At Ardent, Marketing Dollar,
[20:00:53] [backend] and Stars Wellness, I ran 100% performance because budgets needed measurable ROI. But brand was a natural side effect
[20:00:53] [backend] —
[20:00:53] [backend] after six months of Ardent's campaigns, branded searches increased 80%.
[20:00:53] [backend] The test: "If I cut this budget, would revenue drop within 30 days?" Yes means performance. No means brand. Retargeting
[20:00:53] [backend] bridges both — at Marketing Dollar, 35% of conversions involved view-through attribution.
[20:00:53] [backend] I build the performance engine first, prove ROI, earn trust, then introduce brand investment at 15-20% once core campaigns
[20:00:53] [backend] are mature.
[20:00:53] [backend] Q122. How would you approach marketing if you had unlimited budget?
[20:00:53] [backend] COACH: Unlimited doesn't mean undisciplined. Four phases. Still kill what doesn't work.
[20:00:53] [backend] 
[20:00:53] [backend] Leave them thinking: "More budget doesnt change discipline — it accelerates learning."
[20:00:53] [backend] Unlimited budget doesn't mean unlimited spending — it means I'd test aggressively and scale winners without constraint.
[20:00:53] [backend] Phase one, months 1-3: launch $5,000 tests on 8 channels simultaneously — YouTube, TikTok, Reddit, podcasts,
[20:00:53] [backend] programmatic, CTV alongside core Google and Meta. Identify top 3 winners in 90 days.
[20:00:53] [backend] Phase two, months 4-6: hyper-personalization. Instead of one campaign, create segmented campaigns for different user
[20:00:53] [backend] profiles with tailored messaging.
[20:00:53] [backend] Phase three, months 7-9: content and brand investment. Owned channels — video series, podcasts, SEO content — that
[20:00:53] [backend] compound over time.
[20:00:53] [backend] Phase four, months 10-12: advanced data infrastructure. Attribution platforms, customer data warehouses, predictive
[20:00:53] [backend] analytics.
[20:00:53] [backend] Even with unlimited budget I would NOT spray across channels without testing, chase vanity metrics, or abandon
[20:00:53] [backend] performance marketing. Budget increase doesn't change discipline. It accelerates learning.
[20:00:53] [backend] Q123. How do you make decisions when you have incomplete data?
[20:00:53] [backend] COACH: Ardent Milwaukee expansion. Proxy data + worst-case. Reversible vs irreversible model.
[20:00:53] [backend] Leave them thinking: "Calculated decisions with safety nets rather than waiting for perfect information."
[20:00:53] [backend] At Ardent, the CEO wanted to expand from Chicago to Milwaukee with zero data. I launched a $1,000 test based on proxy
[20:00:53] [backend] data and worst-case analysis. Month 1: $280 CPA. Month 3: $210, stabilized at 10-12 conversions/month.
[20:00:53] [backend] My framework: gather proxy data — Chicago at $180 CPA, Milwaukee at 22% of Chicago's population, fewer competitors.
[20:00:53] [backend] Calculate worst-case acceptable loss — even at $300 CPA, 7 patients at $12,000 LTV generates $84,000 from $2,200. Start
[20:00:53] [backend] small with kill criteria. Check week 1, not month 3.
[20:00:53] [backend] Mental model: reversible versus irreversible. Launching a test is reversible — pause tomorrow. Rebranding is irreversible
[20:00:53] [backend] —
[20:00:53] [backend] get more data. For reversible decisions, bias toward action with safety nets.
[20:00:53] [backend] At M.A.S.S., tested TikTok with $200 to learn definitively. Zero conversions, killed, but learned for $200 instead of
[20:00:53] [backend] debating
[20:00:53] [backend] for months.
[20:00:53] [backend] Open with numbers. Show the mechanism. Benchmark it. Bridge to their company.
