[20:03:26] [backend] demand?
[20:03:26] [backend] COACH: Show geo holdout and time-based holdout methods. Reference Stars Wellness 88% lift result.
[20:03:26] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person knows the difference between correlation and causation in marketing data."
[20:03:26] [backend] At Stars Wellness, a geo holdout test proved 88% of conversions were ad-driven — not organic demand. That single test
[20:03:26] [backend] justified scaling the budget from $2K to $6K in January, generating 450 conversions at $13.33 CPA. The incrementality
[20:03:26] [backend] test
[20:03:26] [backend] was the business case.
[20:03:26] [backend] The core problem: conversions happening while ads run doesn't mean ads caused them. People who were going to convert
[20:03:26] [backend] anyway inflate your reported ROAS. If you measure lift incorrectly, you overpay for demand you already had.
[20:03:26] [backend] Five methods I've used. Geo holdout: turn off ads in specific cities, keep ads running everywhere else, compare conversion
[20:03:26] [backend] rates. At Stars Wellness, test cities generated 100 conversions vs 12 in control cities — proving 88% incremental lift.
[20:03:26] [backend] Timebased holdout: turn off all ads for one to two weeks and measure conversion drop. At Ardent, pausing Google Ads for
[20:03:26] [backend] one
[20:03:26] [backend] week dropped weekly consultations from 12 to 4 — proving 67% of consultations were ad-driven. Brand search analysis: if
[20:03:26] [backend] campaigns drive awareness, branded search volume increases. At M.A.S.S., launching Instagram campaigns produced a
[20:03:26] [backend] 60%
[20:03:26] [backend] increase in branded Google searches — proving awareness lift. New vs returning customer split: at Marketing Dollar, 95%
[20:03:26] [backend] of
[20:03:26] [backend] 
[20:03:26] [backend] ad-driven conversions were first-time patients — direct incremental proof. Attribution window analysis: if conversions
[20:03:26] [backend] happen immediately after click, ads may be capturing existing demand; if they take 7-14 days, ads are influencing a
[20:03:26] [backend] decision.
[20:03:26] [backend] I'd design and run an incrementality test at We Inspect within the first 90 days. The result should drive budget allocation
[20:03:26] [backend] decisions, not
[20:03:26] [backend] assumptions.
