[20:03:26] [backend] built it manually without a dedicated data team.
[20:03:26] [backend] The problem it solves: GA4 knows traffic and micro-conversions. Salesforce knows which leads became revenue. Without
[20:03:26] [backend] connecting them, you can only calculate lead-based ROAS — and lead-based ROAS is often a misleading number. The
[20:03:26] [backend] campaigns that look best on CPL are not always the campaigns generating the most closed revenue. I've seen that gap lead
[20:03:26] [backend] to
[20:03:26] [backend] wrong budget allocation decisions.
[20:03:26] [backend] The connection mechanism: UTM parameters. Every paid ad carries UTM source, medium, campaign, and a unique click
[20:03:26] [backend] ID.
[20:03:26] [backend] When a lead converts and enters Salesforce, those UTM values get captured in custom fields on the Lead object. When they
[20:03:26] [backend] close, the revenue is attributable back to the exact campaign and ad group that sourced them.
[20:03:26] [backend] Tableau as the integration layer: I pull Salesforce closed-won data with UTM attribution via API or scheduled export, join
[20:03:26] [backend] it
[20:03:26] [backend] with GA4 campaign performance data on the UTM identifier, and produce a single table showing: campaign → clicks →
[20:03:26] [backend] leads → qualified actions → closed revenue → true ROAS. One source of truth, not three competing dashboards.
[20:03:26] [backend] At Ardent, this took roughly 4 hours of setup per month but fundamentally changed the quality of every optimization
[20:03:26] [backend] decision. We stopped improving CPL and started improving cost-per-closed-consultation — which is what the business
[20:03:26] [backend] measured success against. That difference in optimization target produced the 28% cost reduction and 40% booking
[20:03:26] [backend] improvement.
[20:03:26] [backend] That's the infrastructure I'd build here — and I'd build it in the first 30 days, not after six months of flying blind.
