[20:35:53] [backend] Q28. Walk me through how you structure a Google Ads campaign from scratch.
[20:35:53] [backend] COACH: 5-layer hierarchy. Show intent-based ad group segmentation — not product-based. Reference Ardent.
[20:35:53] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person builds architecture, not just campaigns."
[20:35:53] [backend] At Ardent, campaign architecture built this way drove 28% CPC reduction and 35% consultation increase within 3 months.
[20:35:53] [backend] The structure isn't complicated — but most accounts get it wrong because they organize by product, not intent.
[20:35:53] [backend] Five decisions in order. Campaign level: objective first, then budget allocation, geo-targeting, and network selection. For
[20:35:53] [backend] Ardent's IVF clinics, search network with geo-radius targeting around each clinic location. Ad group level: organized by
[20:35:53] [backend] intent signal, not product category. "IVF clinic near me" vs "IVF success rates" vs "IVF cost" represent completely
[20:35:53] [backend] different
[20:35:53] [backend] buying stages — each needs distinct ad copy and landing pages. Ad level: 3-4 responsive search ads per group testing
[20:35:53] [backend] different value props. Landing pages: 1:1 message match — if the ad says "Free Consultation," the landing page headline
[20:35:53] [backend] says "Free Consultation." Measurement: conversion tracking live before a dollar is spent.
[20:35:53] [backend] Here's the benchmark that matters: most campaigns group keywords by product category, producing Quality Scores of 5-6.
[20:35:53] [backend] Intent-based segmentation consistently produces QS 8-9 — which translates to 35-50% lower CPCs on the same keywords.
