[20:35:53] [backend] Attribution across Google and Meta is where most paid media managers make expensive mistakes — and where I've built a
[20:35:53] [backend] distinct advantage.
[20:35:53] [backend] The core problem: both platforms claim credit for the same conversion. Meta reports view-through conversions; Google
[20:35:53] [backend] reports last-click. Add up their reported ROAS figures and you'll almost always get a higher total than your actual revenue.
[20:35:53] [backend] Companies make budget allocation decisions on these numbers and wonder why cutting one platform tanks the other's
[20:35:53] [backend] performance.
[20:35:53] [backend] My solution: GA4 as the source of truth for cross-channel decisions, not platform-native dashboards. Platform dashboards
[20:35:53] [backend] are
[20:35:53] [backend] useful for within-platform optimization — match types, creative performance, audience testing. They're not reliable for
[20:35:53] [backend] telling you whether Google or Meta deserves more budget.
[20:35:53] [backend] For incremental contribution: holdout tests. Suppress Meta for a defined audience segment for two weeks and watch
[20:35:53] [backend] whether
[20:35:53] [backend] Google Search volume and conversion rates hold. They typically drop — which is evidence that Meta's upper-funnel work
[20:35:53] [backend] is
[20:35:53] [backend] 
[20:35:53] [backend] driving Google-attributable conversions that last-click completely obscures. At Ardent, proving assisted conversion value
[20:35:53] [backend] saved the Meta budget from being cut by a stakeholder who was only looking at last-click data.
[20:35:53] [backend] For day-to-day: data-driven or position-based attribution in GA4 — 40% to first touch, 40% to last, 20% middle. Imperfect,
[20:35:53] [backend] but far more honest than last-click which systematically undervalues upper-funnel channels by 40-60%.
