[20:35:53] [backend] Q63. How do you handle attribution in multi-touch customer journeys?
[20:35:53] [backend] COACH: Show the platform bias problem and GA4 as source of truth. Reference the Ardent assisted conversion example.
[20:35:53] [backend] Leave them thinking: "This person would not lose our Meta budget because of a last-click reporting problem."
[20:35:53] [backend] At Ardent, proving Instagram's assisted conversion value — using multi-touch attribution rather than last-click — saved the
[20:35:53] [backend] Meta budget from being cut. Without that analysis, we would have eliminated a channel responsible for 30% of
[20:35:53] [backend] consultation
[20:35:53] [backend] pipeline because last-click gave it zero credit.
[20:35:53] [backend] The platform bias problem: both Google and Meta claim credit for the same conversion. Last-click attribution gives 100%
[20:35:53] [backend] credit to the final touchpoint. In a journey where the user saw a Meta awareness ad, searched Google three days later, and
[20:35:53] [backend] converted from Search — last-click gives Google 100% and Meta zero. The actual contribution was shared. Most budget
[20:35:53] [backend] allocation decisions made on last-click data systematically undervalue upper-funnel channels by 40-60%.
[20:35:53] [backend] My approach: data-driven or position-based attribution in GA4 as the primary measurement tool — 40% to first touch, 40%
[20:35:53] [backend] to last, 20% to middle touchpoints. More honest than last-click, and it surfaces the channels doing demand creation work
[20:35:53] [backend] that
[20:35:53] [backend] never gets credit.
[20:35:53] [backend] For cross-platform journeys: UTM parameters on every paid ad, tracked through GA4. At Ardent, I tracked "assisted
[20:35:53] [backend] conversions" — channels that appear in the path but aren't last click. Instagram had low last-click conversions but a high
[20:35:53] [backend] assist rate. That's the evidence that saved the budget.
[20:35:53] [backend] For multi-device journeys: GA4's cross-device reporting showed 35% of conversions at Marketing Dollar involved multiple
[20:35:53] [backend] devices. Without tracking this, mobile's awareness contribution was invisible.
[20:35:53] [backend] I'd set up multi-touch attribution infrastructure at We Inspect before the first demand campaign launches.
